    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d175">

                <pb facs="0177" n="167" xml:id="pg167"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/2414: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador to the Yugoslav Government in Exile</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">MacVeagh</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-01T18:00:00+03:00">April 1, 1944—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 1—3:56 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Yugoslav Series 78. See my Yugoslav Series 77, March 31, 6 p.m. Yesterday General
                    Wilson sent Mr. Maniu a message urging that the Rumanians take action in line
                    with Soviet proposals to him and Antonescu and stating, in reply to their
                    inquiries as to what British and American help they can expect, that as an
                    immediate measure he is prepared to make powerful air attacks on such targets as
                    they suggest. Details concerning this message, its prior submission to the
                    Soviet Government and the latter’s reply, are given in Lord Moyne’s telegram
                    125, March 31, to Washington.</p>
                <p>A message dated yesterday was received from Maniu today stating that Antonescu
                    “in principle” is won over to the Allied cause and is awaiting urgently the
                    repetition of Soviet conditions through Cretzeanu (see my Yugoslav Series 76 of
                    March 30, 5 p.m.).</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacVeagh</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
